  <section class="hero">
    <h1>What Does “Farflung” Mean?</h1>
    <p class="lede">One word, three spellings, and a geography game. Here’s the short version of both.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>The word</h2>
    <p><strong>Farflung</strong> (also spelled <em>far-flung</em> or <em>far flung</em>) is an adjective describing something a great distance away, or spread over a very large area: a <em>farflung island</em>, a <em>farflung network of outposts</em>, <em>far-flung corners of the world</em>. It’s literally “far” + “flung” — thrown a long way.</p>
    <p>Typical usage:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>“She spent a decade reporting from far-flung places.”</li>
      <li>“The kingdom’s far-flung provinces rarely saw the capital.”</li>
      <li>“A farflung family scattered across four continents.”</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Dictionaries usually list it as <em>far-flung</em>; style guides vary, and all three spellings appear in the wild. In casual writing — and in the game’s branding — the unhyphenated <em>farflung</em> is common.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>The game</h2>
    <p><a href="https://farflung.game" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Farflung</a> is a free daily geography game built on that idea: each day you get five prompts, pin a country on the world map for each, and the <em>rarer</em> your answers, the further you travel. The game’s top rarity tier is called <strong>Farflung</strong> — the answer almost nobody thinks of, thrown furthest of all.</p>
    <p>Example: for a prompt like “name a country where German is an official language”, Germany counts, but Belgium — a farflung answer — carries you much further.</p>
    <p>New here? Read the <a href="/how-to-play/">how-to-play guide</a>, check the <a href="/tiers/">rarity tiers</a>, and get spoiler-free clues on the <a href="/">today page</a> before you play.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Quick answers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Is it “farflung” or “far-flung”?</strong> Both are accepted; dictionaries prefer the hyphen, the game uses one word.</li>
      <li><strong>Is “farflung” one word or two?</strong> As an adjective it’s one (optionally hyphenated); “far” and “flung” split only in other constructions.</li>
      <li><strong>What is the Farflung game?</strong> A daily five-prompt world-map quiz where rare answers win — see <a href="/how-to-play/">the rules</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
